# mac-plus - a Macintosh Plus (1986): the map, the VIA, the video, the SCC,
# the clock chip, the keyboard and the disk.
#
#   rsemu run mac-plus --media macrom=Mac-Plus.ROM
#   rsemu run mac-plus -p ram=4M --media macrom=Mac-Plus.ROM
#   rsemu run mac-plus --media macrom=Mac-Plus.ROM --vnc :5900
#   rsemu run mac-plus --media macrom=Mac-Plus.ROM --floppy System-Startup.dsk
#
# The ROM chimes, sizes memory, runs its memory test, initialises the SCC and
# the IWM, reads the clock chip and writes its parameter RAM back, finishes the
# keyboard's Model Number handshake and settles into asking it for a key every
# quarter second, draws the Macintosh's grey desktop with the arrow cursor over
# it, and then puts the **insert-disk icon** - the floppy with a question mark -
# in the middle of the screen, with the 60.15 Hz tick chain running. From about
# thirteen seconds on the icon **blinks** and the ROM polls the drive's
# disk-in-place line six to eight times a second; with an 800K image in the
# drive it spins the motor up, **reads the track**, decodes the two boot
# blocks off it, finds no system on them and puts the disk back out. What it
# cannot do yet is boot, which wants Apple system software on the image -
# `docs/platforms/mac-plus.md` has the ledger of what is missing and of what is
# ruled out. The picture reaches a person through `--vnc`, which serves the
# video circuit's scanout.
#
# Written from *Guide to the Macintosh Family Hardware* (Apple Computer, 2nd
# edition) - chapter 3 for the address map, the overlay and the clock chip,
# chapter 7 for the keyboard, chapter 9 for the disk, the VIA chapter for the
# port assignments, the video chapter for the raster - from the Synertek SY6522
# and Zilog Z8530 data sheets, and from Apple's patent 4,564,941 for the disk
# checksum. **No Macintosh emulator source was consulted and the ROM was not
# disassembled**; where the documents were ambiguous the answer came from
# watching which addresses a real ROM touches (ROADMAP.md 1, CLAUDE.md). No
# byte of any Apple ROM or disk image is in this repository.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Big-endian, and it is the board's job to say so
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A 68000 reads a word high byte first, and rsemu carries byte order per
# *region* rather than per initiator, so nothing checks that a machine put a
# 68000 on a big-endian map: get it wrong and every word is swapped and the
# reset vector points somewhere absurd. `m68k-mini.machine` has the long
# version. The space says so, and every `map` below either repeats it or names
# a region that declares it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The address map
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   $00 0000 - $3F FFFF   memory, repeating - or the ROM while the overlay is up
#   $40 0000 - $4F FFFF   ROM, repeating every 128 KiB
#   $58 0000 - $5F FFFF   SCSI (an NCR 5380) - not modelled; floats
#   $60 0000 - $7F FFFF   memory while the overlay is up; nothing afterwards
#   $80 0000 - $9F FFFF   the SCC, read
#   $A0 0000 - $BF FFFF   the SCC, written
#   $C0 0000 - $DF FFFF   the IWM
#   $E8 0000 - $EF FFFF   the VIA
#
# Two things about that map are worth saying out loud.
#
# **The ROM is at $400000 and also at zero.** The processor's first two
# fetches are the two longwords at $000000 and $000004, and the second of them
# is a program counter pointing into $40xxxx - so the machine only starts
# because the overlay puts the ROM under the vector table, and only keeps going
# because the same ROM answers at its own address as well.
#
# **Both the ROM and main memory repeat.** A 128 KiB ROM answers all through
# the megabyte its select decodes, because the socket carries no address line
# above its own; memory does the same because the DRAM is given only the
# address lines its own depth needs, so a board with less than 4 MiB on it
# leaves A20/A21 out of the decode. That fold is what makes the boot screen
# work: the ROM draws the insert-disk icon through a pointer near the top of
# the 4 MiB window rather than through `ScrnBase`, and folded that lands on the
# middle of the screen buffer whatever the memory size. Unfolded it lands in
# nothing on anything but a 4 MiB machine. `src/dev/mac/glue.rs` has the
# argument, which is a measurement rather than a reading of the schematic.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is deliberately not mapped, and what an access to it does
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# $50 0000 - $57 FFFF   nothing
# $58 0000 - $5F FFFF   the SCSI controller, which this build has no model of
# $F0 0000 - $FF FFFF   the phase-read space
#
# An access to any of them **completes and floats**: the space's `unassigned`
# policy is `open-bus`. A Macintosh Plus has no bus-error timeout on these
# ranges - /DTACK is generated by the address decoder for everything the board
# claims and the processor simply reads whatever the bus is holding - and the
# MC68000 user's manual (section 5.4) leaves /BERR to external circuitry a
# board may omit. A `fault` policy here turns the ROM's first probe of a chip
# this build does not have into a bus error, and the startup code has nowhere
# to take it.

machine "mac-plus" {
  # -- how much memory -------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # A Plus shipped with 1 MiB in four SIMM sockets and took 2 MiB or 4 MiB.
  # Whatever this is, it repeats through the 4 MiB window, and the video
  # circuit hangs the screen buffer below the *top of what is fitted* - so
  # `-p ram=4M` moves the screen as a real expansion does, and the ROM finds
  # the size for itself.
  param ram = 1M

  # How many mechanisms are on the disk cable: 1 is a Plus with its internal
  # drive alone, 2 adds the external one at the back.
  param drives = 1

  # -- the crystal -----------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # One oscillator: 15.6672 MHz, the dot clock. The processor is half of it,
  # 7.8336 MHz, and the VIA is the 68000's E clock, a tenth of the processor.
  # Every ratio on this board is therefore exact (ROADMAP.md 4.2), which is
  # what lets the video circuit's 60.15 Hz interrupt be dot-exact against the
  # processor rather than approximately right.
  param master = 15667200
  osc   clk    = master Hz

  # -- the bus ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # 24 bits. A 68000 has 23 address pins and A0 is implied by the two data
  # strobes, so the chip drives a 16-megabyte space and the top byte of a
  # 32-bit address never leaves the package - which is exactly why the VIA is
  # at $EFE1FE and not somewhere a 32-bit machine would put it. Empty
  # addresses float rather than fault: see the block above the machine.
  space mem { width = 24, endian = "big", unassigned = open-bus }

  # -- the processor ---------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `model` defaults to 68000, which is what a Plus has.
  object cpu "cpu.m68k" {
    clock  = clk / 2
    space  = mem
    engine = "interp"
  }

  # -- memory ----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `image` names a media slot: whoever runs the machine binds the bytes
  # (`--media macrom=Mac-Plus.ROM`), and nothing below host/ opens a file. A
  # Plus ROM is 128 KiB. Some ROM files carry trailing bytes that are not part
  # of the ROM at all - the one this board was developed against has 7,504 of
  # them - so trim the image to 128 KiB first; the socket has nowhere to put
  # the rest (`docs/platforms/mac-plus.md`).
  object dram "ram" { size = ram }
  object rom  "rom" { size = 128K, image = "macrom" }

  # -- the address decoder ---------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/glue.rs`. It names its two sources as *objects* rather
  # than addresses, because RAM's only address on this machine is zero and so
  # is the decoder's own: there would be nothing to look up.
  object glue "mac.glue" { rom = rom, ram = dram }

  # -- the VIA ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/via.rs`. On the E clock, one tenth of the processor -
  # which is `clk / 20`, written against the crystal like every other clock
  # here. Its sixteen registers are 512 bytes apart because the board puts the
  # chip's register selects on A9-A12, and the block repeats every 8 KiB
  # through the half-megabyte the chip select decodes.
  object via "mac.via" { clock = clk / 20 }

  # -- the video circuit -----------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/video.rs`. It has no registers and no memory: it reads
  # main memory in step with the beam, which is why it names `dram` and why
  # nothing maps it. Its clock is the dot clock, one tick a pixel.
  object video "mac.video" { clock = clk, ram = dram }

  # -- the sound circuit -----------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/sound.rs`. Like the video circuit it has no registers and
  # no memory of its own: it reads a buffer in main memory, which is why it
  # names `dram`. The Guide's sound chapter gives one byte a horizontal scan
  # line, so **its clock is the line rate** - `clk / 704`, one tick a sample -
  # and 370 samples is one frame of the raster, which is why a waveform whose
  # period divides 370 (the ROM's startup chime has a period of 37) is seamless
  # across a frame boundary. 15 667 200 / 704 is 22 254.5454 Hz, not a whole
  # number of hertz, which is why it is written as a division of the crystal
  # rather than in hertz (ROADMAP.md 4.2).
  #
  # `record` is off here and the host's `--record-audio` switches it on, for
  # the reason `amiga-a500.machine` leaves Paula's off: a machine nobody is
  # listening to should not fill a ring - and here it should not pay for the
  # 22 254 scheduler rounds a second that reading each byte at the tick its
  # line scanned it costs, either.
  object snd "mac.sound" { clock = clk / 704, ram = dram }

  # -- the disk controller ---------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/iwm.rs`. Its sixteen soft switches are 512 bytes apart on
  # the same A9-A12 decode as the VIA's registers, and the block repeats every
  # 8 KiB through the two megabytes its select covers. `$DFE1FF` is the
  # published base and it is an *odd* address: the IWM sits on the low byte
  # lane where the VIA sits on the high one.
  #
  # One mechanism, the internal drive. `-p drives=2` adds the external one.
  #
  # Its clock is the medium's rather than the board's: **one tick is one bit
  # cell**, and 500 kHz is what the IWM's own cell time works out at. It is a
  # separate oscillator because nothing on this board divides down to it - the
  # rate a disk was written at is a property of the disk, not of the computer
  # reading it - and routing it through the 15.6672 MHz tree would be an
  # inexact ratio pretending to be an exact one (ROADMAP.md 4.2).
  #
  # **How fast the disk turns is not this number.** It is this number divided
  # by how many cells a cylinder is round, and a cylinder is
  # `gcr::SECTOR_CELLS` per sector: 394 rpm on a twelve-sector outer cylinder,
  # 591 on an eight-sector inner one. A Macintosh Plus ROM checks the spindle
  # speed against the drive's tachometer before it will read a byte and refuses
  # anything outside 386 to 401 rpm on the outer zone, so a cylinder of the
  # wrong length is the same thing as an unreadable disk. `src/dev/mac/gcr.rs`
  # carries that measurement.
  #
  # `image` names the `floppy` media slot: `--floppy disk.dsk` or
  # `--media floppy=disk.dsk`, a raw 400K/800K image or a DiskCopy 4.2
  # container. An unbound or empty slot is an empty drive, which is the
  # ordinary case. A 1.44 MB image is **refused by name**: a Plus has an IWM
  # and an 800K drive, and 1.44 MB needs the SWIM (`src/dev/mac/disk.rs`).
  param  bitrate = 500000
  osc    diskbit = bitrate Hz
  object iwm "mac.iwm" { drives = drives, clock = diskbit, image = "floppy" }

  # -- the clock chip --------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/rtc.rs`. A watch crystal beside it, and the date it starts
  # at is a property rather than the host's clock: a device may never read real
  # time (CLAUDE.md, Determinism), so two runs of this machine see the same
  # date. `pram` is a flat battery by default - every byte $FF - which is what
  # a real ROM finds when the battery has gone, and it writes its own defaults
  # over the top.
  param  rtcdate = "2026-01-01T00:00:00"
  osc    rtcxtal = 32768 Hz
  object rtc "mac.rtc" { clock = rtcxtal, time = rtcdate }

  # -- the keyboard ----------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/keyboard.rs`. It has a crystal of its own rather than a
  # division of the computer's, and one tick of it is one microsecond, because
  # every interval the Guide gives for this link is in microseconds: 400 us a
  # bit going out, 330 us coming back.
  param  kbdrate = 1M
  osc    kbdclk  = kbdrate Hz
  object kbd "mac.keyboard" { clock = kbdclk }

  # -- the SCC ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/scc.rs`. Two windows, because the board has no read/write
  # pin for the chip and decodes the direction from the address instead.
  object scc "mac.scc" { }

  # -- the memory map --------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # RAM is *not* mapped directly. It is reached through `glue.low`, which
  # decides per access whether the ROM or the RAM answers - one decoder in
  # front of both, rather than two mappings that would have to be swapped from
  # inside the VIA write that clears the overlay.
  map mem 0x000000 size 0x400000 = glue.low  { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0x600000 size 0x200000 = glue.high { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0x400000 size 0x100000 = mirror(rom) { endian = "big" }

  # Only A1 and A2 reach the SCC, so each window repeats every eight bytes
  # through the two megabytes its select covers. $9FFFF8 is channel B's
  # control address in the read window and $BFFFF9 is its write address, and
  # both land on offset 0 of a copy.
  map mem 0x800000 size 0x200000 = mirror(scc.read)  { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0xA00000 size 0x200000 = mirror(scc.write) { endian = "big" }

  # The VIA's four register selects are on A9-A12 and nothing above them, so
  # its 8 KiB block repeats sixty-four times through $E80000-$EFFFFF. $EFE1FE,
  # the address every published Macintosh equate is written against, is
  # register 0 of the last copy, the one at $EFE000; $EFFFFE is register 15 of
  # it, which is `vBufA`.
  map mem 0xE80000 size 0x80000 = mirror(via) { endian = "big" }

  # The same decode for the IWM, over the two megabytes its own select covers.
  # $DFE1FF is switch 0 of the copy at $DFE000.
  map mem 0xC00000 size 0x200000 = mirror(iwm) { endian = "big" }

  # -- wiring ----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # "PA4  ROMOVERLAY" (the Guide's VIA port-assignment table). Active high, and
  # out of reset the VIA's port A is all inputs, so the pin shows the pull-up
  # and the ROM is at zero before any code has run. Software clears the overlay
  # by making PA4 an output and writing a zero, once it has sized memory.
  wire via.pa4 -> glue.overlay { pull = "up" }

  # Interrupts. A Macintosh has no priority encoder: the VIA's /IRQ goes
  # straight to IPL0 and the SCC's /INT straight to IPL1, so the VIA alone is
  # level 1, the SCC alone is level 2, and both at once really is level 3.
  # Which is why the SCC letting go of /INT is load-bearing rather than
  # cosmetic: nothing between the chip and the processor can mask it.
  # That is the hardware, not a simplification. (IPL2 is the programmer's
  # switch, which is a button this board does not have.)
  wire via.irq -> cpu.ipl0
  wire scc.irq -> cpu.ipl1

  # The video circuit's two blanking signals. Vertical blanking is the VIA's
  # CA1 and is the 60.15 Hz interrupt the whole Macintosh tick chain hangs off;
  # `H4` is PB6, which software polls rather than taking an interrupt from.
  #
  # Both nets carry a `pull`, and that is not decoration. The realize sweep
  # (ROADMAP.md 4.3) refreshes a *resolved* net so every sink learns its idle
  # level; a per-sink net has no level of its own, so a driver whose output
  # already matches a fresh source's default announces nothing and the VIA
  # comes up holding the pull-up its pin has with nothing wired to it. The
  # first blanking interval then looks like an edge that has already happened.
  # `H4` really is a pulled-up line; vertical blanking idles low.
  wire video.vblank -> via.ca1 { pull = "down" }
  wire video.hblank -> via.pb6 { pull = "up" }

  # "PA6  PAGE2": which of the two screen buffers the beam reads. Low selects
  # the alternate one.
  wire via.pa6 -> video.page2 { pull = "up" }

  # The clock chip's three wires, from the Guide's Table 3-11: "bit 2 output
  # rtcEnb, 0 = RTC is enabled; bit 1 output rtcClk, RTC's data-clock line;
  # bit 0 in or out rtcData, RTC's serial data line". The data line is two
  # statements because both ends drive it, exactly as the keyboard's is, and
  # all three are open-collector with pull-ups.
  #
  # The one-second interrupt is CA2, and the Guide describes it as an interrupt
  # request the VIA latches; the chip drives it as a square wave and the VIA
  # takes one edge of it a second, which is the same thing.
  wire via.pb2  -> rtc.enb  { pull = "up" }
  wire via.pb1  -> rtc.clk  { pull = "up" }
  wire via.pb0  -> rtc.data { pull = "up" }
  wire rtc.data -> via.pb0
  wire rtc.irq  -> via.ca2  { pull = "up" }

  # The keyboard. "The Keyboard Data line is bidirectional, driven at some
  # times by the computer, and at other times by the keyboard, but the Keyboard
  # Clock line is driven only by the keyboard" - the Guide, chapter 7 - so the
  # clock is one statement and the data line is two, one per driver, which is
  # how a net with a driver at each end is written. Both are open-collector
  # with pull-ups, which is what lets either end pull and neither end fight.
  #
  # The data line's pull is load-bearing for the same reason the blanking
  # nets' are: the computer's "I am ready" signal is the line going *low*, and
  # a line with no pull has no high to come back to.
  wire kbd.clk  -> via.cb1 { pull = "up" }
  wire kbd.data -> via.cb2 { pull = "up" }
  wire via.cb2  -> kbd.data

  # The sound circuit's four gates, from the Guide's VIA port-assignment
  # tables: "PA0-PA2  sound volume", "PA3  SNDPG2, 0 = alternate sound buffer"
  # and "PB7  SNDENB, 0 = sound enabled". All four are plain VIA outputs, and
  # all four idle at their pull-ups while port A and port B are still all
  # inputs - which is a muted speaker reading the main buffer, exactly what a
  # Macintosh does before any code has run.
  wire via.pa0 -> snd.snd0   { pull = "up" }
  wire via.pa1 -> snd.snd1   { pull = "up" }
  wire via.pa2 -> snd.snd2   { pull = "up" }
  wire via.pa3 -> snd.sndpg2 { pull = "up" }
  wire via.pb7 -> snd.sndenb { pull = "up" }

  # "PA5  SEL": the fourth address bit of the disk drive's own register file.
  # It lives on the VIA rather than on the IWM because the IWM has only three
  # `CA` lines to spend on it, and the mechanism needs four.
  wire via.pa5 -> iwm.sel { pull = "up" }
}
